1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,501
A camp in the hills of southern Afghanistan,
in the autumn of the year one thousand

2
00:00:05,501 --> 00:00:10,202
and thirty. A young king wakes up to find that
his army belongs to someone else. By

3
00:00:10,202 --> 00:00:14,847
evening, he is a prisoner in a fortress. And a
letter is already on its way to his

4
00:00:14,847 --> 00:00:15,300
brother.

5
00:00:25,650 --> 00:00:30,337
Welcome to The Honest Historian. I'm Mehdi
Rahmani, and this is Folio Two. Last

6
00:00:30,337 --> 00:00:34,847
week, we stood with the crowd in Balkh and
watched a proud vizier die on the

7
00:00:34,847 --> 00:00:39,356
gallows. Today, I want to go back a little, to
the months that made that day

8
00:00:39,356 --> 00:00:39,890
possible.

9
00:00:40,670 --> 00:00:45,510
Because before Masud could punish anyone, he
had to become Sultan. And in the year

10
00:00:45,510 --> 00:00:50,409
one thousand and thirty, that was not at all
certain. He had a rival, and his rival

11
00:00:50,409 --> 00:00:51,590
was his own brother.

12
00:01:06,870 --> 00:01:11,773
Let me introduce the family. Sultan Mahmud of
Ghazni ruled a huge empire, from the

13
00:01:11,773 --> 00:01:16,616
edge of India in the east to the cities of
central Iran in the west. His capital,

14
00:01:16,616 --> 00:01:21,459
Ghazni, sits in the mountains of what is now
Afghanistan. Mahmud had two sons who

15
00:01:21,459 --> 00:01:23,970
matter for our story. Masud was the elder.

16
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:29,543
Muhammad was the younger, and some sources say
the two were born in the same year,

17
00:01:29,543 --> 00:01:30,770
perhaps even twins.

18
00:01:31,550 --> 00:01:36,290
For a long time, Masud was the chosen heir.
His father made him governor of Herat,

19
00:01:36,290 --> 00:01:40,915
the beautiful city in the west of today's
Afghanistan, when he was still a young

20
00:01:40,915 --> 00:01:45,597
man. Masud led armies. He fought in the
mountains of Ghur. He was bold, generous,

21
00:01:45,597 --> 00:01:48,430
quick to act, and very popular with the
soldiers.

22
00:01:49,210 --> 00:01:54,112
But father and son did not get along. Mahmud
was a hard, suspicious ruler, and Masud

23
00:01:54,112 --> 00:01:58,663
was a prince who did not like to be told what
to do. Near the end of his life,

24
00:01:58,663 --> 00:02:03,506
Mahmud changed his mind. He gave the western
lands, the newly conquered city of Ray

25
00:02:03,506 --> 00:02:05,490
and the lands around it, to Masud.

26
00:02:05,770 --> 00:02:11,354
And he named Muhammad, the quieter son, with
far less experience of war, as his heir

27
00:02:11,354 --> 00:02:12,950
to the throne in Ghazni.

28
00:02:13,730 --> 00:02:18,950
In other words, he sent the strong son far
away, and kept the weaker son close.

29
00:02:34,230 --> 00:02:39,374
At the end of April, in the year one thousand
and thirty, Sultan Mahmud died in

30
00:02:39,374 --> 00:02:39,830
Ghazni.

31
00:02:40,610 --> 00:02:45,562
Masud was in Isfahan, in the middle of Iran,
at that time. He was busy with his own

32
00:02:45,562 --> 00:02:50,455
plans. He had been thinking of marching
further west, to Hamadan, and perhaps even

33
00:02:50,455 --> 00:02:54,990
to Baghdad, the seat of the caliph. Ghazni was
nearly a thousand miles away.

34
00:02:55,770 --> 00:03:00,160
Meanwhile, in the capital, the great men of
the court made a quick decision. They

35
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:04,712
followed the old Sultan's will. They brought
Muhammad from Guzganan, the province he

36
00:03:04,712 --> 00:03:07,530
governed in the north, and placed him on the
throne.

37
00:03:08,310 --> 00:03:12,909
Three men stood behind him. The first was the
commander of the army, a Turkish

38
00:03:12,909 --> 00:03:17,743
general named Ali Qarib, whom Beyhaqi usually
calls Hajib Ali, which means Ali the

39
00:03:17,743 --> 00:03:22,696
Chamberlain. The second was Yusuf, the
brothers' own uncle. And the third was a name

40
00:03:22,696 --> 00:03:25,290
you know from last week: the vizier Hasanak.

41
00:03:25,570 --> 00:03:30,340
So when Hasanak said, if Masud ever becomes
king, let them hang me, he was not

42
00:03:30,340 --> 00:03:34,010
speaking in the abstract. He was standing on
the other side.

43
00:03:34,790 --> 00:03:39,730
For a few months, Muhammad was Sultan. But, as
Beyhaqi lets us see, the real

44
00:03:39,730 --> 00:03:43,110
business of the state was in the hands of
Hajib Ali.

45
00:03:58,390 --> 00:04:03,937
Masud did not rush blindly toward Ghazni. He
did something clever first. He wrote to

46
00:04:03,937 --> 00:04:04,730
his brother.

47
00:04:05,510 --> 00:04:09,907
We know about this letter because Masud
mentions it himself, later, in a private

48
00:04:09,907 --> 00:04:14,415
note to the generals. He says that he sent his
brother a message of condolence for

49
00:04:14,415 --> 00:04:18,922
their father, and of congratulation, and of
advice. And he claims that if Muhammad

50
00:04:18,922 --> 00:04:22,330
had accepted that advice, he would have shared
power with him.

51
00:04:22,610 --> 00:04:26,895
Whether Masud really meant it, we cannot know.
But it allowed him to say,

52
00:04:26,895 --> 00:04:31,650
afterwards, that he had offered peace first,
and that his brother had refused it.

53
00:04:32,430 --> 00:04:37,154
Then Masud began to move. He gave up the idea
of Baghdad. He marched east, from

54
00:04:37,154 --> 00:04:42,117
Isfahan to Ray, then to Nishapur, the great
city of Khorasan. There, it is said, an

55
00:04:42,117 --> 00:04:46,961
envoy of the caliph in Baghdad reached him
with a document recognizing him as the

56
00:04:46,961 --> 00:04:50,250
ruler of his father's lands. That was powerful
support.

57
00:04:50,530 --> 00:04:56,058
Soldiers and officials began to drift toward
him, one by one. By the autumn, Masud

58
00:04:56,058 --> 00:05:00,170
was in Herat, his old city, close to the heart
of the empire.

59
00:05:15,450 --> 00:05:20,525
Now, at last, Muhammad marched out of Ghazni
with his army to face his brother. He

60
00:05:20,525 --> 00:05:25,661
got as far as a place called Tiginabad, in the
dry country of southern Afghanistan,

61
00:05:25,661 --> 00:05:27,270
near the old town of Bust.

62
00:05:28,050 --> 00:05:30,950
And there, his army simply left him.

63
00:05:31,730 --> 00:05:36,641
It was not a battle. No swords were drawn.
Hajib Ali and the other commanders

64
00:05:36,641 --> 00:05:41,871
quietly sent word to Masud that they were now
his men. Even the uncle, Yusuf, went

65
00:05:41,871 --> 00:05:42,190
over.

66
00:05:42,470 --> 00:05:46,826
On the third day of the month of Shawwal, in
the Islamic year four hundred and

67
00:05:46,826 --> 00:05:51,293
twenty one, which fell in early October of one
thousand and thirty, Muhammad was

68
00:05:51,293 --> 00:05:55,928
deposed. He was taken, with his family and his
servants, to a fortress at Tiginabad

69
00:05:55,928 --> 00:05:58,050
called Kuhtiz, and placed under guard.

70
00:05:58,830 --> 00:06:03,694
And here is where Beyhaqi becomes precious to
us. Because he does not just tell us

71
00:06:03,694 --> 00:06:08,676
that this happened. He copies the actual
letter that the generals sent to Masud that

72
00:06:08,676 --> 00:06:09,210
very day.

73
00:06:09,990 --> 00:06:14,812
It is a remarkable document. The men who had
put Muhammad on the throne five months

74
00:06:14,812 --> 00:06:19,693
earlier now explain, very politely, that God's
decree always runs the way God wills.

75
00:06:19,693 --> 00:06:24,515
Muhammad's reign, they say, was only a
temporary arrangement. The rightful king has

76
00:06:24,515 --> 00:06:28,350
now appeared. They had only been obeying the
late Sultan's orders.

77
00:06:28,630 --> 00:06:33,190
And would His Majesty please forgive them, and
tell them what to do with his

78
00:06:33,190 --> 00:06:33,670
brother.

79
00:06:34,450 --> 00:06:39,299
If you have ever read a statement from a
company that has just changed sides, you

80
00:06:39,299 --> 00:06:42,890
know this tone. It has not changed much in a
thousand years.

81
00:06:58,170 --> 00:07:02,951
Beyhaqi also gives us a glimpse of Muhammad
himself, the young man left behind in

82
00:07:02,951 --> 00:07:07,732
the fortress. For two or three days, we are
told, he was lost in sorrow and would

83
00:07:07,732 --> 00:07:12,336
not speak. A few days later, envoys came up to
the fortress with a letter from

84
00:07:12,336 --> 00:07:12,690
Masud.

85
00:07:12,970 --> 00:07:18,005
Muhammad would stay in Kuhtiz, honorably, with
his household, but under guard. When

86
00:07:18,005 --> 00:07:20,250
he read it, he grew visibly troubled.

87
00:07:21,030 --> 00:07:26,029
And one of the envoys, a wise old official,
told him to be patient and accept what

88
00:07:26,029 --> 00:07:31,029
God had decided. Then he said three short
Persian words. Budani bude ast. What was

89
00:07:31,029 --> 00:07:32,370
meant to be, has been.

90
00:07:33,150 --> 00:07:37,270
It is a gentle sentence. It is also a door
closing.

91
00:07:52,550 --> 00:07:57,015
Now, what about the kingmaker, Hajib Ali? He
had made one Sultan and then unmade

92
00:07:57,015 --> 00:08:01,703
him. He must have felt very safe. Masud had
written to him with warm praise and told

93
00:08:01,703 --> 00:08:06,168
him to come to Herat with the army, the
elephants and the treasury, and promised

94
00:08:06,168 --> 00:08:09,070
that the affairs of the state would be in his
hands.

95
00:08:09,850 --> 00:08:14,479
So Ali came. He arrived in Herat at the
beginning of November. The next morning, he

96
00:08:14,479 --> 00:08:18,941
went to court. He knelt, kissed the Sultan's
hand, and offered rich gifts. Masud

97
00:08:18,941 --> 00:08:23,626
spoke with him for a long time, about his
brother, about money, about the army. Then

98
00:08:23,626 --> 00:08:25,690
Ali was asked to wait in a side room.

99
00:08:25,970 --> 00:08:30,721
And there, Beyhaqi tells us, about thirty
servants seized him, stripped him of his

100
00:08:30,721 --> 00:08:35,589
robe and his boots, and put him in heavy
chains. His baggage train was plundered the

101
00:08:35,589 --> 00:08:36,110
same day.

102
00:08:36,890 --> 00:08:42,481
Masud, it seems, had learned a simple lesson.
A man who can give away a throne once

103
00:08:42,481 --> 00:08:44,030
can give it away again.

104
00:08:44,810 --> 00:08:49,723
Beyhaqi does not cheer. He says that only God
knows what was truly in Ali's heart.

105
00:08:49,723 --> 00:08:54,516
And he adds a warning to anyone who trusts
this deceitful world, because fortune

106
00:08:54,516 --> 00:08:58,350
turns, and the man who stands highest is often
the next to fall.

107
00:09:13,630 --> 00:09:17,967
Masud spent that winter in Balkh, the old city
in the north of Afghanistan. He

108
00:09:17,967 --> 00:09:22,472
arrived in the middle of December. Beyhaqi
describes the scene with real joy. The

109
00:09:22,472 --> 00:09:27,032
city, he writes, looked like a bride dressed
for her wedding. The nobles came with

110
00:09:27,032 --> 00:09:30,090
gifts, the court relaxed, and there was music
and wine.

111
00:09:30,370 --> 00:09:35,843
The struggle was over. The next year, Masud
entered Ghazni as Sultan, and the people

112
00:09:35,843 --> 00:09:36,690
welcomed him.

113
00:09:37,470 --> 00:09:42,093
But a new reign brings its own accounts to
settle. The old supporters of Muhammad

114
00:09:42,093 --> 00:09:46,829
were not forgotten. And in Balkh, not long
after, one of them would be led out to a

115
00:09:46,829 --> 00:09:48,370
gallows. You know his name.

116
00:10:03,650 --> 00:10:07,010
So what does Beyhaqi want us to see in all
this?

117
00:10:07,790 --> 00:10:12,148
I think he wants us to see how power really
moves. Not always with battles and

118
00:10:12,148 --> 00:10:16,450
heroes, but with letters, with silence, with
men quietly changing sides on an

119
00:10:16,450 --> 00:10:21,088
ordinary autumn day. He shows us a brother who
offered peace, and perhaps meant it,

120
00:10:21,088 --> 00:10:25,390
and perhaps did not. A general who made kings
and forgot that kings remember.

121
00:10:25,670 --> 00:10:30,510
And a young man in a fortress, being told that
what was meant to be, has been.

122
00:10:31,290 --> 00:10:36,237
Beyhaqi served Masud for many years. He
admired him in many ways. But he does not

123
00:10:36,237 --> 00:10:41,246
hide the cold side of this victory. That
honesty is the whole reason we still read

124
00:10:41,246 --> 00:10:41,490
him.

125
00:10:42,270 --> 00:10:47,229
If there is one lesson here, it is this: those
who change sides for power should

126
00:10:47,229 --> 00:10:49,770
remember that power can change sides too.

127
00:11:05,050 --> 00:11:09,522
Next week, we'll climb up to that fortress.
The Brother in the Fortress: what

128
00:11:09,522 --> 00:11:14,400
happened to Amir Muhammad, the Sultan who
reigned for five months, and how his story

129
00:11:14,400 --> 00:11:14,690
ends.

130
00:11:15,470 --> 00:11:20,218
If you enjoyed this story, follow the show so
you don't miss the next one, and share

131
00:11:20,218 --> 00:11:24,854
it with someone who loves history. I'm Mehdi
Rahmani. This episode was retold from

132
00:11:24,854 --> 00:11:29,602
the Persian original of the History of
Beyhaqi, and narrated with an AI rendering of

133
00:11:29,602 --> 00:11:31,750
my own voice. Thank you for listening.

